35086	{"artifactId":"ISSUE-e980ffbb6dd346e7","entry":{"id":"migrated-ISSUE-e980ffbb6dd346e7-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-12T09:25:11.268Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"issue","stage":"issue","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"c1","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"runInsrcMcpStdio is defined at src/mcp/server.ts:1254 and, inside that function, calls registerBuiltinTools() at :1272, registerBuiltinDataDrivers() at :1273 and registerWorkflowRunners() at :1274, with a comment at :1261 saying it mirrors what the main daemon does at boot.","evidence":"Every anchor checks out against the source. `read src/mcp/server.ts:1254 → export async function runInsrcMcpStdio(): Promise<void> {` shows where the function is defined. `read src/mcp/server.ts:1261 → // path). Mirrors what the main daemon does at boot -- see` confirms the comment at the cited line. The three calls are at the cited lines, all after the 1254 definition and close together in the same function body: `src/mcp/server.ts:1272: registerBuiltinTools();`, `src/mcp/server.ts:1273: registerBuiltinDataDrivers();` and `src/mcp/server.ts:1274: registerWorkflowRunners();`. The grep also backs up the \"mirrors the daemon\" claim: the daemon makes the same calls at `src/daemon/index.ts:283: registerBuiltinTools();` and `src/daemon/index.ts:289: registerBuiltinDataDrivers();`, and calls workflow runners lazily at `src/daemon/workflow-rpc.ts:175: registerWorkflowRunners();`. The other grep matches are in tests and docs, so they don't change the result.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"All five line anchors and all three symbol anchors resolve to the content the premise claims. No change is needed."}},{"claimId":"c2","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"code_class_locate (src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-locate.ts) reads the graph by calling getGraphStore() directly at line 214 (`const s = await getGraphStore();`).","evidence":"The read of src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-locate.ts:214 returns `const s = await getGraphStore();`. That matches the premise word for word. The same file registers the tool as `id: 'code_class_locate'` at line 59, so line 214 is part of the code_class_locate builtin. The grep /const s = await getGraphStore\\(\\)/ found no other match under src/daemon/tools. The other source matches are test helpers: src/db/graph/__tests__/helpers/store-child.ts:114 and :151, and src/db/graph/__tests__/store.test.ts:285 and :306. The rest are matches inside the ISSUE.md document.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise: line 214 of class-locate.ts calls getGraphStore() directly, inside the code_class_locate builtin. No change is needed."}},{"claimId":"c3","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"code_class_references (src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts) reads the graph by calling getGraphStore() directly at line 184 (`const store = await getGraphStore();`).","evidence":"The direct read of src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:184 returns `const store    = await getGraphStore();`. That matches the quoted text in the premise; the only difference is the extra spaces used for alignment. The grep /const store\\s+= await getGraphStore\\(\\)/ finds the same line, `src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:184`. The grep /id:\\s*'code_class_references'/ finds `src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:70: id: 'code_class_references',`, so this file is the one that registers the code_class_references tool. Both the anchor and the claim are confirmed.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise. The file:line resolves to the cited direct getGraphStore() call, inside the file that registers code_class_references. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"c4","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"getGraphStore (src/db/graph/store.ts:416, doc comment :410 'Lazy-init the env + all sub-DBs.') lazily opens the LMDB environment in whatever process calls it, keeps it open as a module-level singleton, and does not check which process is calling.","evidence":"Both anchors resolve as the premise says. The read of src/db/graph/store.ts:416 returns `export async function getGraphStore(): Promise<GraphStore> {`, and the read of src/db/graph/store.ts:410 returns ` * Lazy-init the env + all sub-DBs.`. Together these confirm that the exported entry point opens the LMDB env and its sub-DBs lazily, in whatever process calls it. The sibling ISSUE excerpt (bug-when-another-process-holds-live…/ISSUE.md:57) records that store.ts:439 reads `root = open({` inside the same function, so the env is opened in the calling process. The search for process-identity checks (/process\\.(pid|title|argv)|INSRC_DAEMON/) returned no match in src/db/graph/store.ts. That search was truncated at 50 matches, so this absence is suggestive rather than conclusive, but it is consistent with the claim that the caller is not checked. The module-level singleton caching was not shown directly in a source read. Two earlier verified reviews of this same claim (ISSUE.md:60 and :118) say the handle is cached in module state, and nothing in the evidence contradicts that.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchors and the lazy-open behaviour are confirmed by direct reads. The singleton caching and the missing caller check are consistent with the evidence and with earlier verified reviews. Nothing is wrong that would change the fix this premise supports, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"c5","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"getReadOnlyTools in src/analyze/context/tool-surface.ts throws ReadOnlyToolRegistryMismatch (class declared ~:155, doc at :171) when any allowlist entry has no registered tool, so an empty registry makes it throw.","evidence":"Every anchor checks out against the source. `src/analyze/context/tool-surface.ts:155` declares `export class ReadOnlyToolRegistryMismatch extends Error`, so \"~:155\" is exact. The read of `src/analyze/context/tool-surface.ts:171` returns the doc comment \" * Throws ReadOnlyToolRegistryMismatch if any allowlist entry has no\". The function itself is declared at `src/analyze/context/tool-surface.ts:179` as `export function getReadOnlyTools(): ToolDefinition[]`, right after that doc comment. The class body, captured in the S008 measurement record (t06.json:854), builds its message as \"Read-only tool surface references unregistered tool(s): ${list} ... or the registry was not populated before getReadOnlyTools() was called\". So the throw condition covers the case where the registry is empty. If no tools are registered, every allowlist entry is missing. The function would throw unless the allowlist were empty, and nothing in the evidence suggests that. The empty-registry conclusion follows directly from the documented condition.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The source confirms all three anchors: the class at :155, the doc comment at :171 and the function at :179. The empty-registry conclusion follows from the documented throw condition and from the error message's 'registry was not populated' wording. No change needed."}},{"claimId":"c9","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/analyze/context/driver.ts imports getReadOnlyTools from './tool-surface.js' at line 86 and calls `const tools = getReadOnlyTools();` in its tool loop at line 773.","evidence":"Both anchors match the source exactly. The read of src/analyze/context/driver.ts:86 returns `import { getReadOnlyTools } from './tool-surface.js';`, and the read of src/analyze/context/driver.ts:773 returns `\tconst tools = getReadOnlyTools();`. The grep for the import pattern matches driver.ts:86, and the grep for `const tools = getReadOnlyTools()` matches driver.ts:773. The other matches are in docs or in tool-surface.test.ts. The live-run capture (S008 t06.json:2385) shows this call sitting inside `async function runToolLoop(...)`, which supports the claim that it is in the tool loop.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is confirmed at both cited lines, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p-allowlist","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"code_class_locate and code_class_references are both members of the analyze read-only tool allowlist (READ_ONLY_TOOL_IDS in src/analyze/context/tool-surface.ts), so they are offered to the analyze tool loop (freeform.probe) in the MCP process.","evidence":"The direct reads confirm the premise. `src/analyze/context/tool-surface.ts:126` reads `'code_class_locate',` and `:127` reads `'code_class_references',`, and the greps place both entries in the READ_ONLY_TOOL_IDS allowlist in that file. The snapshot test pins both ids as well: `src/analyze/context/__tests__/tool-surface.test.ts:110` (`'code_class_locate'`) and `:111` (`'code_class_references'`) sit inside EXPECTED_READ_ONLY_TOOL_IDS, which is declared at `:61` and asserted equal to READ_ONLY_TOOL_IDS at `:131-132`. Both tools are registered under exactly those ids: `src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-locate.ts:59` (`id: 'code_class_locate'`) and `src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:70` (`id: 'code_class_references'`). Because the ids match, getReadOnlyTools resolves both from the registry; the t06.json body shows it looping over READ_ONLY_TOOL_IDS and calling `registered.get(id)`. Both tools are therefore offered on the analyze tool surface. The freeform.probe tool loop uses this surface, which `docs/daemon.md:408` describes as \"freeform.probe's tool-loop turns\". The premise's claim that this happens in the MCP process is supported only indirectly: the earlier ISSUE.md row c5 states that the MCP registers builtins because getReadOnlyTools throws ReadOnlyToolRegistryMismatch. That claim is consistent with the evidence and is not contradicted.","action":"None needed; the evidence confirms the premise.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise: both ids are at tool-surface.ts:126-127 inside READ_ONLY_TOOL_IDS, the snapshot test pins them, and tools are registered under those exact ids. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p-builtin-openers","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"Among the builtin tools under src/daemon/tools/builtins/, exactly two call getGraphStore() directly: code_class_locate (class-locate.ts:214) and code_class_references (class-references.ts:184).","evidence":"Both anchors are confirmed. The read of src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-locate.ts:214 returns `const s = await getGraphStore();`. The read of src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:184 returns `const store    = await getGraphStore();`.\n\nThe \"exactly two\" claim cannot be checked from the evidence. The /getGraphStore\\(\\)/ grep is TRUNCATED at 50 matches, and its last returned match is src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-locate.ts:214. So the cap falls inside the builtins range, not after it. Every builtin file that sorts after code/class-locate.ts is missing from the grep output. That includes class-references.ts itself, which is seen only through the direct read, plus the rest of code/* and the data/, db/, gh/, graph/ and other builtin directories.\n\nThe earlier ISSUE.md review rows (ISSUE.md:53, :116) say the cap hit in `src/db/__tests__`, after the whole builtins range. That no longer holds for this run: matches in docs/ and src/daemon/__tests__ use up the cap first.\n\nThe second grep (/from '.*db/graph/store\\.js'/) is also truncated before src/daemon/tools/. It shows no builtin importer and cannot show that none