y rather than enumerate callers.","evidence":"The evidence confirms each part of the premise. The read of src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:105 returns `const { getGraphStore } = await import('../../db/graph/store.js');`. The read of src/workflow/code-review/freshness.ts:92 returns the same dynamic import. The dynamic-import grep matches both lines. The only other source hit is the test src/daemon/__tests__/backup.test.ts:164, which imports backupGraphStore. The wrapper claim also holds: the getGraphStore() grep shows direct calls inside src/config/store.ts at :167, :199, :210 and :250, so anything that calls those functions opens the store indirectly. The static-import grep shows even more wrapper modules than the artifact names, so the list of callers is open. These are src/db/doc-summaries.ts:27, src/db/exploration-cache.ts:31, src/db/graph/edges.ts:21, src/db/graph/ids.ts:27 and src/db/graph/traversal.ts:31. There is also a daemon-side call at src/daemon/index.ts:572. The getGraphStore() grep stops at its 50-match cap, so further callers likely exist. All of this supports the conclusion that the fix should guard the store boundary, because a list of callers cannot be shown to be complete.","action":"None needed; the premise checks out. Optionally, the artifact could name the extra src/db wrappers (doc-summaries.ts, exploration-cache.ts, graph/edges.ts, graph/ids.ts, graph/traversal.ts) as more evidence that the list of openers is open. That would strengthen the case for guarding the store boundary.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise checks out against the evidence. The cited anchors resolve exactly, and the open set of callers is confirmed, with more wrapper modules than the artifact lists. No edit is needed. The extra wrappers are an optional addition, not a correction."}},{"claimId":"c6","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"realGroundingDeps is defined at src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:104 and at :105 dynamically imports getGraphStore from '../../db/graph/store.js'.","evidence":"The evidence backs both parts of the premise. `read src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:104` returned `export async function realGroundingDeps(): Promise<GroundingDeps> {`. The grep /export async function realGroundingDeps/ finds exactly one definition in source, at that same line; its other 3 matches are in docs/standalone/.../ISSUE.md. `read src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:105` returned `const { getGraphStore }              = await import('../../db/graph/store.js');`. That is a dynamic import of getGraphStore from '../../db/graph/store.js'. The dynamic-import grep also matches this line, src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:105. The only differences from the premise are alignment spaces.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is verified as written. Both cited lines resolve to the claimed entity and the claimed import, so no edit is needed. Note: the same dynamic import also appears at src/workflow/code-review/freshness.ts:92. That matters only if the artifact claims grounding.ts is the only place that opens the store; it does not affect this citation."}},{"claimId":"c10","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/mcp/server.ts:50 imports DEFAULT_DEPS and handleCodeReviewStep from './code-review-step/handler.js', and src/mcp/code-review-step/handler.ts:38 imports from '../../workflow/code-review/grounding.js', so grounding.ts is in the MCP server's import closure.","evidence":"Both cited lines match what the premise says. Reading `src/mcp/server.ts:50` gives `import { DEFAULT_DEPS as CODE_REVIEW_STEP_DEFAULT_DEPS, handleCodeReviewStep } from './code-review-step/handler.js';`. That is a static top-level import, and the premise names the right symbols; `DEFAULT_DEPS` is imported under the alias `CODE_REVIEW_STEP_DEFAULT_DEPS`. Reading `src/mcp/code-review-step/handler.ts:38` gives `import { assembleDiffCodeReviewGrounding, DiffUnavailableError } from '../../workflow/code-review/grounding.js';`, which is also a static top-level import. The grep searches find the same two lines in source. Their only other source hit is `src/daemon/__tests__/code-review-rpc.test.ts:19`, a test file that has nothing to do with the MCP import chain. Following the two static imports in order, server.ts → handler.ts → grounding.ts, puts grounding.ts in the import closure of server.ts. The premise is limited to the \"MCP server's import closure\", meaning server.ts. So the fact that `src/bin/insrc-mcp.ts` loads server.ts with a dynamic import does not affect this claim.","action":"None needed; the premise is confirmed by the evidence. One optional precision fix: the premise could mention that `DEFAULT_DEPS` is imported under the alias `CODE_REVIEW_STEP_DEFAULT_DEPS`.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both static-import hops match the source exactly at the cited lines, so the artifact needs no correction."}},{"claimId":"p-handler-diff-only","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The MCP code-review-step handler only calls the diff-based grounding (assembleDiffCodeReviewGrounding) and never calls realGroundingDeps; realGroundingDeps is called only from src/daemon/index.ts and src/workflow/code-review/runner.ts.","evidence":"The grep for /realGroundingDeps/ was not truncated: it returned 14 matches. In source, it finds only the definition at `src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:104` (`export async function realGroundingDeps(): Promise<GroundingDeps> {`) and two callers. The first is `src/daemon/index.ts:752-753` (`await realGroundingDeps()`). The second is `src/workflow/code-review/runner.ts:47`, which imports it, with the call at `:148` (`assembleCodeReviewGrounding(repoPath, changedFiles, await realGroundingDeps())`). The other 8 matches are in docs/ markdown. No match in `src/mcp/` refers to realGroundingDeps. The handler imports only the diff-based grounding: `read src/mcp/code-review-step/handler.ts:38` → `import { assembleDiffCodeReviewGrounding, DiffUnavailableError } from '../../workflow/code-review/grounding.js';`. It wires that function as a seam at `handler.ts:87` (`readonly assembleDiffGrounding: typeof assembleDiffCodeReviewGrounding;`) and `:124` (`assembleDiffGrounding: assembleDiffCodeReviewGrounding,`). One more match, `src/daemon/code-review-rpc.ts:25/142`, also uses assembleDiffCodeReviewGrounding and not realGroundingDeps, so it does not contradict the premise. The evidence confirms both halves of the premise.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Verified against the complete realGroundingDeps grep (not truncated) and the handler.ts:38 import read. No edit is needed. One caveat: this shows that the handler does not call realGroundingDeps directly. It does not show whether runner.ts is ever loaded inside the MCP process, which is a separate question about the import closure."}},{"claimId":"c11","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/code-review/freshness.ts:92 dynamically imports getGraphStore from '../../db/graph/store.js'.","evidence":"The read of `src/workflow/code-review/freshness.ts:92` returned `const { getGraphStore }    = await import('../../db/graph/store.js');`, which is exactly what the premise says. The dynamic-import grep also matched this line in source (`src/workflow/code-review/freshness.ts:92`), next to the similar import at `src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts:105`. The quoted line differs from the source only in alignment whitespace.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the cited line, so no change is needed."}},{"claimId":"c12","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"freshness.ts is called only from the daemon: src/daemon/index.ts:770 dynamically imports computeCodeReviewFreshness and realFreshnessGraphDeps from '../workflow/code-review/freshness.js'.","evidence":"The read of src/daemon/index.ts:770 matches the premise exactly: `const { computeCodeReviewFreshness, realFreshnessGraphDeps } = await import('../workflow/code-review/freshness.js');`. The grep for `code-review/freshness\\.js` finds that one source import; the other matches are in docs/standalone/.../ISSUE.md. The grep for `realFreshnessGraphDeps` finds only its definition (src/workflow/code-review/freshness.ts:91) and its uses at src/daemon/index.ts:770-771. The grep for `computeCodeReviewFreshness` finds src/daemon/index.ts:770 and :774, the definition and doc comment in freshness.ts (lines 9, 31, 58), and src/workflow/code-review/__tests__/freshness.test.ts, which imports the function at line 9 and calls it at lines 34–96. Those test imports are not production callers, so the claim that only the daemon calls it holds for production code.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise: src/daemon/index.ts:770 is the only production importer of freshness.ts. The only other caller is the unit test file (freshness.test.ts), which does not affect what gets built. No edit needed."}},{"claimId":"p-config-wrapper","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/config/store.ts wraps getGraphStore(), so code calling it can open the graph store indirectly.","evidence":"The grep /getGraphStore\\(\\)/ finds four direct calls inside src/config/store.ts: src/config/store.ts:167, :199, :210 and :250, each reading `const store = await getGraphStore();`. A direct read of src/config/store.ts:167 returns `\t\tconst store = await getGraphStore();`, which confirms the anchor. Each call sits inside a function body in that module (it is indented within a function, not at module top level). So any caller of those config-store functions reaches getGraphStore() through them. This matches the premise that src/config/store.ts wraps getGraphStore() and is an indirect path to opening the graph store. The evidence does not name the enclosing exported functions, but the premise does not depend on them.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is confirmed by four direct getGraphStore() calls inside src/config/store.ts (:167, :199, :210, :250), so nothing needs to change. One optional improvement: name the exported config-store functions that enclose these calls so the list of indirect openers is complete. The gathered evidence does not identify those functions, so no edit is proposed."}},{"claimId":"p-entry","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"The MCP binary entry src/bin/insrc-mcp.ts imports '../mcp/server.js' and calls runInsrcMcpStdio() (around :44-45), so the builtin registration in runInsrcMcpStdio runs in the MCP process.","evidence":"Both anchors resolve to the code the premise describes. `read src/bin/insrc-mcp.ts:44 → FOUND: const { runInsrcMcpStdio } = await import('../mcp/server.js');` is a dynamic import of the server module. `read src/bin/insrc-mcp.ts:45 → FOUND: await runInsrcMcpStdio();` awaits the call in the binary entry. The greps for `await import('../mcp/server.js')` and `await runInsrcMcpStdio()` each match exactly once in source, at src/bin/insrc-mcp.ts:44 and :45. Every other match is in docs. Because the entry calls runInsrcMcpStdio, whatever that function does, including builtin registration, runs in the MCP process. One small inaccuracy: the premise says the entry 'imports' server.js, but the import is dynamic (`await import(...)`), not a static top-level import. This does not change the ordering claim, because the import resolves before runInsrcMcpStdio() is called.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise. The only nuance is that the import is dynamic, which does not affect whether the registration runs in the MCP process, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"c7","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CLAUDE.md key architectural rule #1 states 'Daemon owns all DB access — CLI, MCP, and the IDE workbench communicate via IPC only.'","evidence":"The read of CLAUDE.md:135 returned \"1. **Daemon owns all DB access** — CLI, MCP, and the IDE workbench communicate via IPC only.\" The grep /Daemon owns all DB access/ matched CLAUDE.md:135 with the same text. The line is item 1 of the numbered \"Key architectural rules\" list, so calling it rule #1 is correct, and the quoted text matches the source word for word. AGENTS.md:135 has the same line, and site/architecture.html:183 states the same rule.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The citation resolves exactly to the quoted rule at CLAUDE.md:135. No change is needed."}},{"claimId":"c8","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"Observed 2026-10-12: lsof showed the Codex-spawned MCP process (node ~/.insrc/daemon/out/bin/insrc-mcp.js, pid 51338) holding graph.lmdb and graph.lmdb-lock while no daemon was running.","evidence":"The premise reports a runtime observation: on 2026-10-12, lsof showed pid 51338 (node ~/.insrc/daemon/out/bin/insrc-mcp.js) holding graph.lmdb and graph.lmdb-lock while no daemon was running. The gathered evidence is only a ripgrep of /graph\\.lmdb/, capped at 50 matches. None of those matches address the claim.\n\n- Some matches are docs that describe the daemon as the process that opens LMDB: design/indexer.html:307 (\"Daemon opens LMDB at ~/.insrc/graph.lmdb\"), docs/daemon.md:164 and docs/daemon.md:178, and docs/plans/graph-storage-lmdb.md:818 (\"Open LMDB env at ~/.insrc/graph.lmdb\").\n- docs/plans/graph-storage-lmdb.md:703 mentions the graph.lmdb-lock reader-table semantics, and the rest are temporary paths from test TAP output.\n- No match comes from the MCP entry point (out/bin/insrc-mcp.js or its src/bin/ / src/mcp/ sources) or from any import chain that would make the MCP process open the graph store directly.\n- The lsof output, pid 51338 and process state are runtime facts. A source probe cannot reproduce them.\n\nThe documented rule that \"Daemon owns all DB access\" is consistent with this being a defect if it happened. But the evidence neither confirms nor contradicts that the MCP process opens LMDB. The premise is therefore unverifiable.","action":"Ground the observation in source by finding the code path that makes the MCP process open graph.lmdb. To do that, trace the imports from src/bin/insrc-mcp.ts and src/mcp/* to getGraphStore or the lmdb open in src/db/graph/store.ts, and cite that file:line in the artifact. Separately, attach the raw lsof output or a reproduction step: start the MCP server with no daemon, then run `lsof +D ~/.insrc | grep graph.lmdb`. That makes the runtime claim checkable. If no such import path exists, re-scope the premise; for example, a stale build in out/ or a different entry point may be what opened the store.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence covers only doc and test mentions of graph.lmdb. Nothing ties the MCP binary to opening the LMDB env, and a runtime lsof observation cannot be verified from the source tree. Any edit to the premise text needs the real import path or a reproduction from a human.","options":["Add a source anchor: cite the src/mcp → src/db/graph/store.ts import chain (file:line) that opens graph.lmdb inside the MCP process.","Attach reproduction evidence: the raw lsof output plus steps (MCP started with no daemon running) as an appendix, and mark the premise as an observed runtime fact.","Reword as a hypothesis ('the MCP process appears to open graph.lmdb directly, violating the rule that the daemon owns all DB access') until the source path is confirmed.","If the open came from a stale out/ build rather than current source, re-scope the premise to rebuild/version drift instead of a code-path defect."]}},{"claimId":"c14","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"With getGraphStore's open options (mapSize 1GiB, maxDbs 32), a second process opening an LMDB env another process holds succeeds (exit 0), so a second holder alone does not stop the daemon from starting; the exit-134 failures are explicitly NOT attributed to this issue.","evidence":"The anchor resolves: src/db/graph/store.ts:416 is `export async function getGraphStore(): Promise<GraphStore> {`, and the open options are at store.ts:441 (`mapSize: mapsizeBytes,`) and store.ts:442 (`maxDbs:  MAX_DBS,`). The evidence does not show what `mapsizeBytes` or `MAX_DBS` equal, so it cannot confirm the premise's \"1GiB\" and \"32\". The only support for the \"second open succeeds (exit 0)\" behaviour is the artifact's own recorded step output: c12 in docs/standalone/bug-when-another-process-holds-live-E20261012f442f10b/ISSUE.md:40 (\"holder open({path, mapSize:1GiB, maxDbs:32}) + put + periodic get; second process same open, read, put, close -> exit 0 …\"), plus the matching c14 in the sibling issue E20261012e980ffbb/ISSUE.md:42. No test in the tree re-runs this. The nearest one, src/db/graph/__tests__/helpers/store-child.ts:111, opens with `maxDbs: 8` and no mapSize, so it does not use getGraphStore's options. The artifact's own c6 row (ISSUE.md:52) still calls it unpinned which native open, LanceDB or lmdb-js, aborts with exit 134. That fits the premise's statement that the exit-134 failures are not attributed to this issue. Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise, but its exact option values and the runtime result cannot be checked here. It is a scoping (negative) claim: it decides what the fix does NOT cover. It does not change what gets built, so no concrete build-breaking failure can be stated.","action":"Read store.ts near lines 430–442 to resolve `MAX_DBS` and `mapsizeBytes` (and how the mapsize config default is computed). Confirm they equal 32 and 1 GiB, or correct the premise text to the real values. Optionally turn the c12 two-process reproduction into a test that uses getGraphStore's real options, so the \"second holder → exit 0\" contract is checked in-tree and not only by a one-off step output.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is consistent with the recorded reproduction and does not change the prescribed fix. The concrete option values come from the reproduction script, not from store.ts, where they are the symbols `mapsizeBytes` / `MAX_DBS` whose values were not in the evidence. A human should either check the constants or state the claim in terms of the symbols.","options":["Verify MAX_DBS === 32 and the default mapsizeBytes === 1 GiB in src/db/graph/store.ts and leave the premise as written.","Reword the premise to cite the symbols rather than literal values: \"With getGraphStore's open options (mapSize: mapsizeBytes, maxDbs: MAX_DBS — store.ts:441-442), …\" and keep c12 as the reproduction citation.","Add an in-tree two-process test (extend src/db/graph/__tests__/helpers/store-child.ts to use getGraphStore's options) so the exit-0 contract is enforced and no longer a one-off observation."]}},{"claimId":"c13","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"Related ledger issues ISSUE-f442f10bd21284a5 (make daemon startup report a held store instead of aborting silently) and ISSUE-0d577569 (daemon loses its LMDB locks when another process closes the store) exist as artifacts the fix must be checked against.","evidence":"Both artifacts exist on the ledger. `docs/standalone/bug-when-another-process-holds-live-E20261012f442f10b/ISSUE.md:1` reads `<!-- insrc:artifact ISSUE-f442f10bd21284a5 -->`. That is the full id named in the premise. Its title, \"Make daemon startup report a held store instead of aborting silently\", is cited at `docs/standalone/bug-insrc-mcp-server-process-opens-E20261012e980ffbb/ISSUE.md:41`. `docs/standalone/defect-installed-daemon-runs-out-lmdb-E202610100d577569/ISSUE.md:1` reads `<!-- insrc:artifact ISSUE-0d5775698fd042a7 -->`. The premise's short id ISSUE-0d577569 is a prefix of that full id. The subject also matches: `bug-when-another-process-holds-live.../ISSUE.md:41` gives it as \"installed daemon runs out of LMDB semaphores (lock loss when another process closes the store)\". The two issues are also linked to each other. f442f10b's ISSUE.md:21 says the abort \"may be the lock-loss state described in ISSUE-0d577569\". Epic E20261011c2ba6fa6 Story S004 (DEF.md:213) takes in ISSUE-0d577569 in full. One small imprecision: the premise uses the full id for the first issue but the short id for the second. The short id is used the same way across the ledger (DEF.md:33, HLD.md:219), so it still resolves to the right issue.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise. ISSUE-0d577569 is the short form of ISSUE-0d5775698fd042a7, and the ledger uses that form everywhere, so nothing needs to change."}}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":3,"low":17},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-12T09:25:11.268Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
